            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0562" n="442" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg442"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">738.3915/234</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the High Commissioner in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">J. H. Russell</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-12-19"><hi rendition="#italic">December 19,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 61</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With further reference to the Department’s
                    instructions of October 30 and November 8, 1922, regarding the boundary between
                    Haiti and the Dominican Republic, there is transmitted herewith, for your
                    information, a copy of a despatch from the American Minister in Santo Domingo
                    enclosing a translation of the reply of the Minister of Foreign Affairs of that
                    country to the Minister’s note of November 15th.<anchor n="78" xml:id="d180-f1"
                    /></p>
                <p>With reference to the second paragraph of Mr. Russell’s despatch, you are
                    requested to communicate the note of the Dominican Minister of Foreign Affairs
                    to the Haitian Government, with a request for a statement from the Haitian
                    Government that it will respect the provisional boundary line laid down in 1912,
                    without prejudice to any rights which either party may claim in the eventual
                    settlement, until such time as a permanent settlement of the boundary question
                    can be effected. You are requested to transmit copies of the Haitian
                    Government’s reply to the Department and also to the American Legation at Santo
                    Domingo.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
